\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

What does fine-tuning change inside a Vision-Language-Action model (VLA)?
VLAs fine-tune large language models on robot demonstrations to produce actions from visual observations and language instructions~\cite{brohan2023_rt2,kim2024_openvla}, but the decoder-only causal mask prevents image tokens from attending to instructions~\cite{wang2025seeing,pei2025rethinking}.
Instruction information must reach action-generating positions through learned attention patterns, yet how fine-tuning reshapes these patterns remains uncharacterized.

Recent behavioral studies reveal that VLAs rely disproportionately on visual input~\cite{grant2026}, with language instructions largely ignored by the action pathway~\cite{haon2025}.
These findings suggest internal specialization but do not explain \emph{how} instruction information reaches the action-generating readout token, \emph{what} attention strategy fine-tuning learns compared to language pretraining alone, or \emph{whether} training creates new representational structure or reconfigures inherited geometry.
Existing analyses have not combined systematic per-layer probing with factorial trained/pretrained/untrained controls, leaving the representational mechanisms of VLA fine-tuning opaque.

We address this gap by decomposing OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024}, a VLA chaining a frozen DinoSigLIP vision backbone~\cite{zhai2023_siglip,oquab2024_dinov2}, an MLP projector, and Llama-2 7B~\cite{touvron2023_llama2}.
We extract representations from each of 32 LLM layers at both image-token and readout-token positions, and from external baselines (DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024_dinov2}, CLIP~\cite{radford2021_clip}).
We compare three LLM conditions (VLA-trained, pretrained Llama-2 base, and randomly initialized) crossed with instruction presence, and apply four complementary analyses: cross-instruction probes for token-level specialization, L1 attention pattern analysis, RSA~\cite{kriegeskorte2008} for geometric alignment, and MLP probes~\cite{alain2017} for functional decodability.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\linewidth,height=2in,keepaspectratio]{figures/fig_teaser.pdf}
\vspace{-4pt}
\caption{\textbf{VLA fine-tuning redistributes instruction attention to entry-exit layers.}
(a)~Architecture overview.
(b)~Instruction-dominant heads per layer (L1 attention $>0.5$). Pretrained Llama-2 peaks at mid-depth; VLA-trained OpenVLA concentrates at L1 and L32. Untrained: zero heads at all layers.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure}

Three findings emerge.
\emph{First}, VLA fine-tuning redistributes instruction attention from mid-depth to entry-exit layers, supported by seven complementary analyses including dose-response and counterfactual ablation (\S\ref{sec:learned_routing}).
\emph{Second}, a $2{\times}2$ trained-vs-untrained comparison across two causal-decoder VLAs---OpenVLA (Llama-2) and TraceVLA (Phi-3-V), which start from dramatically different untrained profiles---reveals a consistent reorganization pattern: both reach similar mid-layer action-decodability peaks after training ($\Delta R^2 = 0.003$), with training effects concentrated at the readout token rather than image geometry (\S\ref{sec:nonmonotonic}).
If routing were purely architecture-specific, different backbones should not arrive at the same functional profile; the observed consistency across two architectures is compatible with VLA training discovering a common organization constrained by the causal-decoder design.
Architecture-expected token specialization (instruction-blind image tokens, perfect readout classification from L1) is verified as a framework sanity check (\S\ref{sec:token_specialization}).

Our contributions are: (i)~evidence that VLA fine-tuning redistributes instruction attention from mid-depth to entry-exit layers, consistent across two causal-decoder VLAs, with L1 as the primary hub and redistribution strength modulated by task instruction-discriminability; (ii)~a consistent reorganization pattern across two causal-decoder VLAs with dramatically different starting points, consistent with training discovering a common functional organization, with effects concentrated at the readout token rather than image geometry; and (iii)~a systematic framework for token-level representational analysis of VLAs with factorial controls, including token specialization as a methodology validation.

